WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:03.710
If there's one thing you
know about puffer fish,

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.007
it's that they can do this.

00:00:06.007 --> 00:00:07.723
When aggravated by a predator,

00:00:07.723 --> 00:00:10.048
they, you know, puff up.

00:00:10.048 --> 00:00:12.830
Some puffers, like the porcupine fish,

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.370
become a bona fide spike ball,

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.570
moving through the water
seemingly out of control.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.670
But if you peer inside a puffer,

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.430
you'll learn that puffing
up isn't the only trait

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.242
that makes these fish one of the most

00:00:26.242 --> 00:00:28.693
threatening creatures in the sea.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.110
Contrary to what it looks like,

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.070
puffer fish are not like balloons.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.428
Because what's normally
inside them isn't air.

00:00:38.428 --> 00:00:39.553
It's water.

00:00:39.553 --> 00:00:40.780
Elizabeth Brainerd: What
they do is they actually

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.710
take water into their mouths
in a big mouthful of water,

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.090
and then they pump it
down into their stomach.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.760
Narrator: That's Elizabeth Brainerd,

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.070
a biologist and puffer fish
expert at Brown University.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.253
Brainerd: And they do that
anywhere 10 or 15 times,

00:00:56.253 --> 00:00:57.960
pump, pump, pump, pump, pump, pump,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.090
until they inflate completely,

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.998
and then they hold it and they'll just be

00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:03.848
a big, spiny ball.

00:01:03.848 --> 00:01:05.750
Narrator: And as you might expect,

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.430
this requires some pretty
sophisticated biology,

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.110
starting with the stomach.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.590
It's made of dozens of tiny folds,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.420
kind of like an accordion.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.410
These folds are important because

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.830
when the stomach fills up with water,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.430
it can expand without rupturing.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.705
And puffer fish expand a lot.

00:01:25.705 --> 00:01:28.160
Up to three times their size.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.050
That's like if an average human
man could inflate his waist

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.120
to a circumference of 3 meters.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.160
But there is a drawback
to these amazing skills.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.690
Brainerd suspects that
puffer fish stomachs

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.320
have actually lost the
ability to digest food,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.810
which means their intestines
have to do all the work.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.190
Brainerd: You know, given
the apparent importance

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:53.800
of this defense mechanism,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.650
they've given up the
advantages of having a stomach

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.720
where some digestion can start.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.090
Narrator: But the stomach?

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.160
It's just one of many bizarre
features inside a puffer.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.670
For example, they have specialized muscles

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.220
that you won't find in most other fish.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.380
Some in their mouth,

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.200
which pump all that
water into their stomach;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.470
some in their esophagus,
to seal off their stomach

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.830
like a drain plug once it's full;

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.960
and some in the base of their bellies,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.390
which contract to squeeze out water

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.200
when they're ready to deflate.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.380
But what you won't find
inside is even more bizarre.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.550
Brainerd: There are a couple characters

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.090
that are really helpful in
their ability to puff up,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.400
and one of those is that
they don't have any ribs,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.900
and another one is they
don't have any pelvis.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.300
Narrator: In other words,
puffers are essentially

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.175
missing bones.

00:02:43.175 --> 00:02:44.192
And that's a good thing,

00:02:44.192 --> 00:02:47.480
because otherwise they'd
get in the way of inflation.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.410
In fact, according to Brainerd,

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.330
if it weren't for these missing bones,

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.138
puffer fish would probably have never

00:02:53.138 --> 00:02:55.430
evolved this way in the first place.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:56.696
And that would be a shame,

00:02:56.696 --> 00:02:59.760
since puffing up really is a good defense.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.100
Consider one old study

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.310
in which researchers
watched birds go fishing.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.400
The birds caught 11 puffer fish,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.470
but they dropped nearly half of them

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.032
because the fish started to inflate.

00:03:11.032 --> 00:03:12.610
But what's more surprising

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.210
is that the birds left with empty beaks

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.210
might have been the lucky ones.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.810
Because puffers have another,

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.570
more potent defense up their sleeves.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.987
Their bodies are laced with a neurotoxin

00:03:23.987 --> 00:03:26.137
called tetrodotoxin.

00:03:26.137 --> 00:03:28.710
It's up to 1,200 times

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.460
more poisonous than cyanide.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.960
So poisonous that one puffer fish

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.520
can kill 30 adult humans.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.510
So poisonous that puffers are reportedly

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:43.450
the second most poisonous
vertebrate in the world,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:45.283
which is why it's also surprising that

00:03:45.283 --> 00:03:46.510
us humans?

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:49.260
We actually eat them. That's right.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.430
In Japan, puffer fish is
actually a delicacy called fugu,

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.390
which only trained chefs can prepare.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.240
And considering that these
fish are basically spike balls

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.040
filled with poison

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.750
and we're still serving
them in restaurants,

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:08.182
they must be seriously delicious.

